{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HnRosbkpaVoMtcVsrKYUCi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tBIBLATEX RULES — Minimal contract (MCP optional)\n2\t\n3\tGoal\n4\t- Produce: (1) a compiled document that uses [@Key] citations, and (2) a BibLaTeX database file biblatex.bib with full entries for every cited key.\n5\t\n6\tCitekeys\n7\t- Valid pattern: ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]{2,64}$ (letters and digits only; start with a letter; unique per document).\n8\t- Key format by number of authors:\n9\t  - 1 author: AuthorYear (e.g., Kramer2015)\n10\t  - 2 authors: Author1Author2Year (e.g., WellsCoppersmith1994, AtkinsonBoore2006)\n11\t  - 3+ authors: FirstAuthorEtAlYear (e.g., BooreEtAl2014, AbrahamsonEtAl2016)\n12\t  - Organization/committee: OrganizationYear (e.g., SSHAC1997, ANCOLD2019)\n13\t- For collisions (same key pattern), append a, b, c: Boore2014a, Boore2014b.\n14\t- Use only the surname portion of each author name (no initials, no given names).\n15\t- This convention is mandatory for cross-session consistency: multiple sessions processing the same paper must produce the same citekey.\n16\t\n17\tMetadata and entries\n18\t- For each token [KB:/DOC:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:], construct a complete BibLaTeX entry with author(s), title, year, and venue/journal/booktitle/publisher as applicable.\n19\t- Put identifiers into fields (doi, url, eprint); never in the citekey. Fields may include punctuation; citekeys must not.\n20\t- Choose an appropriate entry type (@article, @inproceedings, @book, @online, @misc) based on the metadata.\n21\t\n22\tSources of truth\n23\t- Use your knowledge acquired during generation/compilation **only to complete or correct metadata (author, title, year, venue, identifiers) for works that are already identified from upstream citation tokens or existing [@Key] references.**\n24\t- If available, you MAY non-interactively call an MCP or local tool (e.g., RefChecker, Crossref, arXiv, OpenAlex) to verify/enrich metadata for those existing works. Do not prompt the user. Proceed even if external tools are unavailable.\n25\t- Do NOT introduce any new `[@Key]` citations or BibLaTeX entries for works that are not referenced by at least one `[KB:/DOC:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:]` token or existing `[@Key]` in the input.\n26\t\n27\tDeduplication\n28\t- If multiple tokens refer to the same work, use a single citekey and a single entry.\n29\t- Before creating ANY new citekey, search the existing biblatex.bib for the same work (match by DOI, title, or author+year). If found, reuse the exact existing key — do not invent a variant.\n30\t- If biblatex.bib already contains entries from prior slots or sessions, inherit those keys for the same works. Never generate a second key for a work that already has one.\n31\t\n32\tOutput\n33\t- Replace all inline token citations with [@Key] in the compiled document.\n34\t- When extracting citations/keys, ignore citation-like patterns that appear inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented).\n35\t- Create a file biblatex.bib containing exactly one entry per used key.\n36\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb9ae42e-e1d3-4ee7-b51f-914c838e3680","uuid":"6c8830d2-dc79-49f3-81e0-65d1a8dc14e6","timestamp":"2026-03-31T01:03:36.101Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/biblatex.md","content":"BIBLATEX RULES — Minimal contract (MCP optional)\n\nGoal\n- Produce: (1) a compiled document that uses [@Key] citations, and (2) a BibLaTeX database file biblatex.bib with full entries for every cited key.\n\nCitekeys\n- Valid pattern: ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]{2,64}$ (letters and digits only; start with a letter; unique per document).\n- Key format by number of authors:\n  - 1 author: AuthorYear (e.g., Kramer2015)\n  - 2 authors: Author1Author2Year (e.g., WellsCoppersmith1994, AtkinsonBoore2006)\n  - 3+ authors: FirstAuthorEtAlYear (e.g., BooreEtAl2014, AbrahamsonEtAl2016)\n  - Organization/committee: OrganizationYear (e.g., SSHAC1997, ANCOLD2019)\n- For collisions (same key pattern), append a, b, c: Boore2014a, Boore2014b.\n- Use only the surname portion of each author name (no initials, no given names).\n- This convention is mandatory for cross-session consistency: multiple sessions processing the same paper must produce the same citekey.\n\nMetadata and entries\n- For each token [KB:/DOC:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:], construct a complete BibLaTeX entry with author(s), title, year, and venue/journal/booktitle/publisher as applicable.\n- Put identifiers into fields (doi, url, eprint); never in the citekey. Fields may include punctuation; citekeys must not.\n- Choose an appropriate entry type (@article, @inproceedings, @book, @online, @misc) based on the metadata.\n\nSources of truth\n- Use your knowledge acquired during generation/compilation **only to complete or correct metadata (author, title, year, venue, identifiers) for works that are already identified from upstream citation tokens or existing [@Key] references.**\n- If available, you MAY non-interactively call an MCP or local tool (e.g., RefChecker, Crossref, arXiv, OpenAlex) to verify/enrich metadata for those existing works. Do not prompt the user. Proceed even if external tools are unavailable.\n- Do NOT introduce any new `[@Key]` citations or BibLaTeX entries for works that are not referenced by at least one `[KB:/DOC:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:]` token or existing `[@Key]` in the input.\n\nDeduplication\n- If multiple tokens refer to the same work, use a single citekey and a single entry.\n- Before creating ANY new citekey, search the existing biblatex.bib for the same work (match by DOI, title, or author+year). If found, reuse the exact existing key — do not invent a variant.\n- If biblatex.bib already contains entries from prior slots or sessions, inherit those keys for the same works. Never generate a second key for a work that already has one.\n\nOutput\n- Replace all inline token citations with [@Key] in the compiled document.\n- When extracting citations/keys, ignore citation-like patterns that appear inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented).\n- Create a file biblatex.bib containing exactly one entry per used key.\n","numLines":36,"startLine":1,"totalLines":36}}}
