fuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb9ae42e-e1d3-4ee7-b51f-914c838e3680","uuid":"73c74370-5288-440e-87cd-936a42abacaa","timestamp":"2026-03-31T01:03:55.691Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/smoketest/sessions/run-20260330_220324/slot_sources/slot-02.run-20260330_175309_7976.md","content":"## SLOT 2: Highest-density metal and structural relevance\n\nAmong the three metals in the knowledge base, copper exhibits the highest density at $8.96\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$, exceeding both iron at $7.874\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$ and aluminum at $2.70\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$. [KB:metals.md]^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: All three density values are taken directly from the KB; the comparative ranking follows directly and is arithmetically unambiguous from the reported figures.]\n\nHigh density in a structural material implies greater mass per unit volume, a property relevant in applications where inertia, resistance to penetration, or radiation attenuation is required, including counterweights, ballast systems, and protective cladding. The closer atomic packing associated with high-density metals can also contribute to stiffness and wear resistance in load-bearing and high-contact components. The KB provides only density and melting-point values and does not describe mechanical or structural properties; the structural relevance discussed in this paragraph is based on general materials engineering principles not explicitly stated in the KB. [KB:metals.md]^[Confidence: LOW, Rationale: The density values for all three metals are KB-supported; however, the explanation of structural implications extends beyond the KB content, which reports only atomic number, melting point, and density data. The claims regarding stiffness, wear resistance, and application domains rely on ge